<Guide
  slug="codecs-by-platform"
  title="Which codec each video site gives you (YouTube VP9 and AV1, TikTok HEVC) — Super Downloads"
  description="Measured: YouTube serves 1440p and 4K only as VP9 or AV1, TikTok serves 720p and 1080p as HEVC, Vimeo and X serve H.264. What each one means in Premiere Pro and DaVinci Resolve."
  h1="Which codec each video site gives you, and what your editor can open"
  updated="2026-10-27"
>
  <p>Whether a downloaded clip drops into your timeline or throws an error depends on one thing: the codec the site served. Sites don’t say which one you’ll get, so we measured.</p>

  <h2>What the sites serve</h2>
  <p>Video formats offered on October 27, 2026, listed with <a href="https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp">yt-dlp</a> (<code>yt-dlp -F URL</code>) on public videos, no login.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>Up to 1080p</th><th>1440p and 4K</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>YouTube</td><td>H.264 (MP4), VP9 (WebM or MP4), AV1 (MP4)</td><td><strong>VP9 or AV1 only</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>TikTok</td><td><strong>HEVC</strong> at 720p and 1080p; H.264 only at lower sizes</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vimeo</td><td>H.264 at every size offered</td><td>Not in our sample</td></tr>
      <tr><td>X</td><td>H.264 (MP4)</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Instagram, Facebook and LinkedIn need a signed-in browser to list formats, so they aren’t in this table. Sites change their encoding over time; this is a snapshot, re-checked when the date at the top changes.</p>

  <h2>What your editor opens</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Codec</th><th>Premiere Pro</th><th>DaVinci Resolve (Mac)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>H.264</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>HEVC (H.265)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>VP9</td><td>No</td><td>In MP4 or MOV, not in WebM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AV1</td><td>No</td><td>In MP4, MOV or MKV, not in WebM</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Sources: <a href="https://helpx.adobe.com/premiere-pro/using/supported-file-formats.html">Adobe’s supported file formats</a> and <a href="https://documents.blackmagicdesign.com/SupportNotes/DaVinci_Resolve_20_Supported_Codec_List.pdf">Blackmagic’s Resolve 20 codec list</a>. Final Cut Pro reads H.264 and HEVC natively.</p>

  <h2>What it means in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>YouTube at 1080p:</strong> pick the H.264 version and it imports anywhere. Most downloaders pick VP9 or AV1 by default because they are smaller.</li>
    <li><strong>YouTube at 1440p or 4K:</strong> there is no H.264. Convert to H.264 or ProRes before editing in Premiere (commands in <a href="/guides/premiere-pro-webm-av1">Premiere Pro won’t import WebM or AV1</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>TikTok:</strong> HEVC imports, but it is heavier to scrub than H.264 on older Macs. If the timeline stutters, convert or make proxies.</li>
    <li><strong>Vimeo and X:</strong> H.264 already; nothing to do.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Check any file yourself</h2>
  <pre><code>ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 -show_entries stream=codec_name -of default=nw=1 "clip.mp4"</code></pre>
  <p>It prints <code>h264</code>, <code>hevc</code>, <code>vp9</code> or <code>av1</code>. The file extension doesn’t tell you: an <code>.mp4</code> can hold any of the four.</p>

  <h2>How Super Downloads handles it</h2>
  <p>Every file comes out as H.264 with AAC audio in an MP4. When the site already has H.264 at the size you chose, the app keeps it as is; when it doesn’t (YouTube 4K, TikTok HEVC), it converts on your Mac’s hardware encoder.</p>
  <p class="note">Download only video you own, have licensed, that’s shared under Creative Commons, or that you’re otherwise authorized to use.</p>
</Guide>
